INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','Osteoarthritis — Diagnosis & Management','easy','mcq','Which of the following is the most characteristic radiographic finding in osteoarthritis?','["A. Joint space narrowing with osteophyte formation","B. Periarticular osteopenia","C. Subchondral erosions","D. Soft tissue calcification"]','A','Osteoarthritis is characterised radiographically by asymmetric joint space narrowing, subchondral sclerosis, osteophyte formation, and subchondral cysts. Periarticular osteopenia and erosions are features of rheumatoid arthritis.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','Osteoarthritis — Diagnosis & Management','easy','mcq','A 65-year-old woman presents with bilateral knee pain worse on activity and relieved by rest. Crepitus is noted on examination. Which joint pattern is most consistent with primary osteoarthritis?','["A. Wrists and MCPs symmetrically","B. DIP and PIP joints of the hands, knees, and hips","C. Sacroiliac joints and spine predominantly","D. Shoulders and elbows bilaterally"]','B','Primary OA typically affects weight-bearing joints (knees, hips) and the DIP joints (Heberden''s nodes) and PIP joints (Bouchard''s nodes) of the hands. Wrist and MCP involvement suggests RA; sacroiliac involvement suggests spondyloarthropathy.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','Osteoarthritis — Diagnosis & Management','medium','mcq','A 70-year-old man with knee OA has inadequate pain relief with paracetamol. He has no history of peptic ulcer disease or cardiovascular disease. What is the most appropriate next step?','["A. Intra-articular corticosteroid injection","B. Total knee replacement","C. Add a topical or oral NSAID","D. Start methotrexate"]','C','According to stepwise OA management, NSAIDs (topical preferred to reduce GI risk, or oral if no contraindications) are added when paracetamol provides insufficient relief. Joint replacement is reserved for severe, refractory disease. Methotrexate is not used in OA.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','Osteoarthritis — Diagnosis & Management','medium','mcq','Which non-pharmacological intervention has the strongest evidence base for reducing pain and improving function in knee osteoarthritis?','["A. Transcutaneous electrical nerve stimulation (TENS)","B. Acupuncture","C. Ultrasound therapy","D. Aerobic exercise and quadriceps strengthening"]','D','Exercise therapy — particularly aerobic exercise and quadriceps strengthening — has the strongest evidence for improving pain and physical function in knee OA. Muscle strengthening reduces joint loading and decelerates impact. TENS and acupuncture have limited evidence.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','Osteoarthritis — Diagnosis & Management','medium','mcq','Intra-articular corticosteroid injections in OA are most useful in which clinical scenario?','["A. Monoarticular flare with significant joint effusion","B. Bilateral symmetrical joint disease with systemic inflammation","C. Prophylaxis prior to joint replacement surgery","D. Young patients with early-stage OA"]','A','Intra-articular corticosteroids are most appropriate for acute monoarticular flares with significant effusion, providing short-term (weeks) pain relief. They do not modify disease progression and repeated injections may accelerate cartilage damage.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','Osteoarthritis — Diagnosis & Management','hard','mcq','A 68-year-old obese woman with severe medial compartment knee OA has failed conservative management including NSAIDs and physiotherapy. Her BMI is 38 kg/m². Which surgical option specifically addresses unicompartmental disease while preserving bone stock?','["A. Total knee arthroplasty","B. High tibial osteotomy","C. Unicompartmental knee arthroplasty","D. Arthroscopic debridement"]','B','High tibial osteotomy (HTO) corrects varus malalignment and redistributes load away from the medial compartment. It preserves bone stock and is preferred in younger, active patients with unicompartmental disease. Unicompartmental arthroplasty replaces one compartment but does not correct alignment. Arthroscopic debridement has no proven benefit in OA.','universal',false,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','Osteoarthritis — Diagnosis & Management','medium','mcq','Heberden''s nodes in osteoarthritis are bony enlargements located at which joint?','["A. Proximal interphalangeal joints","B. Metacarpophalangeal joints","C. Distal interphalangeal joints","D. Carpometacarpal joints"]','C','Heberden''s nodes are osteophytic enlargements at the distal interphalangeal (DIP) joints, characteristic of hand OA. Bouchard''s nodes occur at the proximal interphalangeal (PIP) joints. Both result from periarticular osteophyte formation.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','Osteoarthritis — Diagnosis & Management','hard','mcq','Which of the following distinguishes inflammatory OA from non-inflammatory OA on synovial fluid analysis?','["A. WBC count >100,000 cells/µL with >90% neutrophils","B. WBC count 2000–50,000 cells/µL","C. Presence of negatively birefringent crystals","D. WBC count <2000 cells/µL with predominantly mononuclear cells"]','D','Non-inflammatory OA synovial fluid typically has WBC <2000 cells/µL with predominantly mononuclear cells, is clear and viscous. Inflammatory conditions (RA, gout, septic arthritis) have higher WBC counts. Negatively birefringent crystals indicate gout.','universal',false,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','Osteoarthritis — Diagnosis & Management','easy','mcq','Which of the following is the first-line analgesic recommended for mild-to-moderate osteoarthritis pain?','["A. Paracetamol (acetaminophen)","B. Oral diclofenac","C. Tramadol","D. Codeine"]','A','Paracetamol is recommended as the first-line analgesic in OA guidelines due to its safety profile. NSAIDs like diclofenac are second-line. Opioids (tramadol, codeine) are generally reserved for refractory pain due to adverse effects and dependence risk.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','Osteoarthritis — Diagnosis & Management','hard','mcq','A 72-year-old man with hip OA awaiting total hip replacement has worsening functional decline over 3 years with severe muscle wasting. According to Harrison''s, what is the implication of delaying surgery until this stage?','["A. Better bone quality for prosthesis fixation","B. Postoperative functional status may not recover to the level achievable with earlier surgery","C. Reduced perioperative cardiovascular risk","D. Reduced risk of prosthesis infection"]','B','Harrison''s explicitly states that patients who suffer for many years until functional status has declined substantially, with considerable muscle weakness, may not recover to the postoperative functional level achieved by those who underwent operation earlier. Early surgical timing optimises outcomes.','universal',true,'{year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','Rheumatoid Arthritis — Diagnosis & Management','easy','mcq','Which of the following best describes the joint distribution pattern of rheumatoid arthritis?','["A. Asymmetric involvement of large joints","B. Predominantly DIP joint involvement","C. Symmetric small joint polyarthritis sparing the DIP joints","D. Axial skeleton predominance with sacroiliac involvement"]','C','RA characteristically presents with symmetric polyarthritis affecting the small joints of the hands (MCPs, PIPs) and feet, notably sparing the DIP joints. This distinguishes it from OA (which involves DIPs) and spondyloarthropathies (which involve the axial skeleton).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','Rheumatoid Arthritis — Diagnosis & Management','easy','mcq','Which antibody is most specific for rheumatoid arthritis?','["A. Antinuclear antibody (ANA)","B. Anti-double-stranded DNA","C. Rheumatoid factor (RF)","D. Anti-cyclic citrullinated peptide (anti-CCP)"]','D','Anti-CCP antibodies have >95% specificity for RA, making them the most specific serologic test. Rheumatoid factor is sensitive but less specific (also elevated in SLE, Sjogren''s, infections). ANA and anti-dsDNA are associated with SLE.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','Rheumatoid Arthritis — Diagnosis & Management','medium','mcq','A 42-year-old woman with newly diagnosed seropositive RA (RF+, anti-CCP+) with moderate disease activity is started on treatment. What is the most appropriate initial DMARD?','["A. Methotrexate","B. Hydroxychloroquine alone","C. Adalimumab (anti-TNF)","D. Prednisolone long-term"]','A','Methotrexate is the anchor DMARD and first-line treatment for RA. It is the standard of care for moderate-to-severe disease and serves as the backbone of combination regimens. Biologics like adalimumab are added for inadequate MTX response. Long-term steroids are not a DMARD strategy.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','Rheumatoid Arthritis — Diagnosis & Management','medium','mcq','A patient with RA on methotrexate develops an inadequate response at 3 months. Which is the most evidence-based next step according to treat-to-target principles?','["A. Switch to hydroxychloroquine","B. Add or switch to a biologic DMARD (e.g., anti-TNF)","C. Add folic acid and wait 6 more months","D. Increase prednisolone dose"]','B','Treat-to-target (T2T) strategy mandates escalation if remission or low disease activity is not achieved within 3–6 months. Adding a biologic DMARD (anti-TNF such as etanercept, adalimumab) to methotrexate is the evidence-based escalation step.','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','Rheumatoid Arthritis — Diagnosis & Management','medium','mcq','Morning stiffness lasting more than 1 hour is a key clinical feature of RA. Which mechanism best explains this finding?','["A. Nocturnal hypoglycaemia causing muscle cramps","B. Periarticular tendon shortening during sleep","C. Accumulation of inflammatory mediators in synovial tissue during rest","D. Subchondral bone remodelling overnight"]','C','Prolonged morning stiffness (>1 hour) in RA results from accumulation of inflammatory cytokines and fluid in the inflamed synovium during rest. Activity disperses these mediators, relieving stiffness — contrasting with OA where stiffness is brief (<30 minutes) and activity worsens pain.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','Rheumatoid Arthritis — Diagnosis & Management','hard','mcq','A 38-year-old woman with severe RA on methotrexate and adalimumab plans to conceive. Which medication change is required?','["A. Stop adalimumab but continue methotrexate","B. Continue both as benefits outweigh risks","C. Switch to leflunomide which is safe in pregnancy","D. Stop methotrexate, consider continuing adalimumab with specialist guidance"]','D','Methotrexate is absolutely contraindicated in pregnancy (teratogenic — folic acid antagonist) and must be stopped ≥3 months before conception. Leflunomide is also contraindicated. TNF inhibitors like adalimumab have relatively more safety data and may be continued into second trimester under specialist guidance. Certolizumab pegol is preferred as it does not cross placenta.','universal',true,'{year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','Rheumatoid Arthritis — Diagnosis & Management','hard','mcq','Which extra-articular manifestation of RA is most strongly associated with high RF titre and represents a vasculitic complication requiring urgent treatment?','["A. Rheumatoid vasculitis causing digital infarcts","B. Rheumatoid nodules","C. Secondary Sjogren''s syndrome","D. Pulmonary nodules"]','A','Rheumatoid vasculitis typically occurs in patients with longstanding seropositive RA and high RF titres. It causes digital infarcts, leg ulcers, mononeuritis multiplex, and visceral ischaemia — requiring immunosuppressive treatment beyond standard DMARDs.','universal',false,'{year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','Rheumatoid Arthritis — Diagnosis & Management','medium','mcq','Prior to starting methotrexate in a patient with RA, which baseline investigation is most important?','["A. Echocardiogram","B. Liver function tests, renal function, FBC, and chest X-ray","C. Bone mineral density scan","D. Synovial biopsy"]','B','Before initiating methotrexate, baseline LFTs (hepatotoxicity risk), renal function (renal excretion), FBC (myelosuppression risk), and CXR (pulmonary toxicity — interstitial lung disease) must be assessed. Folic acid 5 mg/week is co-prescribed to reduce side effects.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','Rheumatoid Arthritis — Diagnosis & Management','easy','mcq','Atlantoaxial subluxation is a serious complication of RA. Which symptom should trigger urgent imaging of the cervical spine?','["A. Wrist pain and swelling","B. Bilateral knee effusions","C. Occipital headache with neck pain and neurological symptoms","D. Elevated ESR"]','C','Atlantoaxial subluxation in RA can compress the spinal cord at C1-C2. Occipital headache, neck pain, and myelopathic signs (weakness, paraesthesia, gait disturbance) should prompt urgent flexion-extension cervical spine X-rays or MRI. This is a pre-anaesthetic consideration before intubation.','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','Rheumatoid Arthritis — Diagnosis & Management','hard','mcq','The 2010 ACR/EULAR classification criteria for RA assigns scores to which four domains?','["A. Joint involvement, imaging, family history, serology","B. Serology, ESR, morning stiffness duration, radiograph erosions","C. Nodules, serology, duration, ANA titre","D. Joint involvement, serology, acute-phase reactants, duration of symptoms"]','D','The 2010 ACR/EULAR criteria score: joint involvement (0–5), serology — RF/anti-CCP (0–3), acute-phase reactants — CRP/ESR (0–1), and symptom duration (0–1). A score ≥6/10 classifies RA. This replaced the 1987 criteria to enable earlier diagnosis.','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','Gout — Acute & Chronic Management','easy','mcq','A 52-year-old man presents with sudden-onset severe pain, redness, and swelling of the right first metatarsophalangeal joint at 2 AM. Serum uric acid is 520 µmol/L. What is the most likely diagnosis?','["A. Gout (podagra)","B. Pseudogout","C. Septic arthritis","D. Reactive arthritis"]','A','Classic podagra — acute gout of the first MTP joint — presents with nocturnal onset of excruciating monoarthritis with erythema, warmth, and tenderness. Elevated serum uric acid supports the diagnosis but is not definitive. Definitive diagnosis requires joint aspiration showing negatively birefringent needle-shaped monosodium urate crystals.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','Gout — Acute & Chronic Management','easy','mcq','According to the Ghana Standard Treatment Guidelines (2017), what is the first-line treatment for acute gout in adults?','["A. Colchicine 500 mcg 6-hourly","B. Diclofenac oral 50 mg 8-hourly or 75 mg 12-hourly","C. Allopurinol 100 mg daily","D. Prednisolone 40 mg daily"]','B','The Ghana STG 2017 recommends Diclofenac oral 50 mg 8-hourly or 75 mg 12-hourly as 1st-line treatment for acute gout in adults (Evidence Rating C). Colchicine 500 mcg 6–12 hourly is 2nd-line. Allopurinol should not be started during an acute attack.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','Gout — Acute & Chronic Management','medium','mcq','According to the Ghana STG 2017, when should allopurinol be initiated in a patient with gout?','["A. Immediately during the acute attack","B. Within 24 hours of symptom onset","C. 1–2 weeks after the acute inflammation has completely settled","D. Only after a second attack occurs"]','C','The Ghana STG 2017 (Caution 22-5) explicitly states: Do not start allopurinol until 1–2 weeks after inflammation has settled to prevent acute flare. However, patients already on allopurinol should continue it and treat the acute attack as usual.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','Gout — Acute & Chronic Management','medium','mcq','What is the target serum uric acid level recommended by the Ghana STG 2017 for patients on urate-lowering therapy?','["A. <600 µmol/L","B. <420 µmol/L","C. <200 µmol/L","D. <300 µmol/L"]','D','The Ghana STG 2017 targets a serum uric acid <300 µmol/L for chronic gout management. Allopurinol is started at 100 mg daily and increased by 100 mg every 2–5 weeks until this target is reached (max dose 900 mg).','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','Gout — Acute & Chronic Management','medium','mcq','Colchicine is used as second-line therapy for acute gout according to the Ghana STG. What is the maximum dose per course?','["A. 6 mg per course","B. 2 mg per course","C. 4 mg per course","D. 10 mg per course"]','A','The Ghana STG 2017 prescribes colchicine 500 micrograms 6–12 hourly until symptoms are relieved, with a maximum of 6 mg per course. The course should not be repeated within 3 days. This limits cumulative toxicity (diarrhoea, myelosuppression, neuromyopathy).','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','Gout — Acute & Chronic Management','medium','mcq','A patient with gout also has stage 3 chronic kidney disease. Which urate-lowering therapy adjustment is required?','["A. Allopurinol is absolutely contraindicated — use febuxostat only","B. Allopurinol dose must be reduced and titrated carefully","C. Colchicine can be given in full dose as it is renally inert","D. No adjustment needed as uric acid excretion is unaffected by CKD"]','B','Allopurinol and its active metabolite oxypurinol are renally excreted. In CKD, doses must be reduced and titrated carefully to avoid toxicity, including hypersensitivity syndrome. The Ghana STG and Harrison''s both note dose adjustment in renal impairment. Colchicine should also be dose-reduced in renal failure.','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','Gout — Acute & Chronic Management','hard','mcq','According to Harrison''s, colchicine anti-inflammatory prophylaxis during urate-lowering therapy initiation should be given until which endpoint?','["A. Until serum uric acid normalises","B. Until tophi resolve completely","C. Until the patient is normouricemic and free of gouty attacks for 6 months, or as long as tophi are present","D. For a maximum of 3 months regardless of disease activity"]','C','Harrison''s states colchicine prophylaxis (0.6 mg once or twice daily) should accompany urate-lowering therapy until the patient is normouricemic and without gouty attacks for 6 months, or as long as tophi are present — since initiation of urate-lowering can paradoxically trigger flares by mobilising urate crystals.','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','Gout — Acute & Chronic Management','hard','mcq','On polarised microscopy of synovial fluid, gout crystals appear as which of the following?','["A. Positively birefringent rhomboid-shaped crystals","B. Non-birefringent amorphous deposits","C. Positively birefringent needle-shaped crystals","D. Negatively birefringent needle-shaped (monosodium urate) crystals"]','D','Monosodium urate crystals in gout are negatively birefringent (yellow when parallel to the compensator axis) and needle-shaped. CPPD crystals (pseudogout) are positively birefringent and rhomboid-shaped. This distinction is critical for the crystal arthropathy differential.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','Gout — Acute & Chronic Management','easy','mcq','Which dietary modification is recommended in the non-pharmacological management of gout according to the Ghana STG 2017?','["A. Reduce purine-rich foods (liver, kidneys, shellfish), alcohol, and increase water intake >2 L/day","B. High-protein diet with red meat daily","C. Ketogenic diet to promote weight loss","D. High-fructose fruit diet to alkalinise urine"]','A','The Ghana STG 2017 non-pharmacological management of gout includes: reducing purine-rich foods (liver, kidneys, shellfish, yeast extracts), reducing alcohol to <14 units/week, avoiding sweetened beverages, and encouraging >2 litres of water daily. Fructose actually raises uric acid levels.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','Gout — Acute & Chronic Management','hard','mcq','Which drug class commonly used in hypertension is cited by the Ghana STG 2017 as a secondary cause of gout by impairing uric acid excretion?','["A. ACE inhibitors","B. Thiazide and loop diuretics","C. Calcium channel blockers","D. Beta blockers"]','B','The Ghana STG 2017 lists thiazide and loop diuretics among drugs that impair uric acid excretion and can precipitate gout, alongside cytotoxic drugs and pyrazinamide. Clinicians should review medications in patients presenting with gout, particularly those on antihypertensives.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','Osteoporosis — Screening & Management','easy','mcq','Which investigation is considered the gold standard for diagnosing osteoporosis?','["A. Plain X-ray of the lumbar spine","B. Serum alkaline phosphatase","C. Dual-energy X-ray absorptiometry (DEXA scan)","D. Bone biopsy"]','C','DEXA scan measures bone mineral density (BMD) and is the gold standard for diagnosing osteoporosis. A T-score of ≤−2.5 at the hip or lumbar spine defines osteoporosis; −1.0 to −2.5 defines osteopenia. Plain X-ray is insensitive — only detects bone loss >30%.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','Osteoporosis — Screening & Management','easy','mcq','At what T-score threshold does osteoporosis treatment with bisphosphonates become indicated in a postmenopausal woman?','["A. T-score ≤−1.0","B. T-score ≤−1.5","C. T-score ≤−3.5","D. T-score ≤−2.5"]','D','A T-score ≤−2.5 on DEXA at the hip or lumbar spine meets WHO criteria for osteoporosis. Pharmacological treatment (bisphosphonates as first-line) is indicated at this threshold, or earlier if the FRAX 10-year fracture probability exceeds local thresholds.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','Osteoporosis — Screening & Management','medium','mcq','Which bisphosphonate regimen is most commonly used for osteoporosis and requires specific administration instructions?','["A. Oral alendronate 70 mg weekly, taken fasting with plain water and remaining upright for 30 minutes","B. IV zoledronic acid once every 5 years","C. Oral risedronate 150 mg daily with food","D. Oral etidronate 400 mg daily with milk"]','A','Alendronate (70 mg weekly) is a widely used oral bisphosphonate. It must be taken fasting, with 200 mL plain water, 30–60 minutes before any food or other medications, and the patient must remain upright for 30 minutes to prevent oesophageal irritation and maximise absorption.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','Osteoporosis — Screening & Management','medium','mcq','The FRAX tool calculates the 10-year probability of fracture. Which of the following is NOT a clinical risk factor included in the FRAX algorithm?','["A. Previous fragility fracture","B. Serum calcium level","C. Parental history of hip fracture","D. Smoking and alcohol use"]','B','The FRAX tool incorporates: age, sex, BMI, previous fracture, parental hip fracture, current smoking, glucocorticoid use, rheumatoid arthritis, secondary osteoporosis, alcohol ≥3 units/day, and BMD (optional). Serum calcium is not a FRAX variable.','universal',false,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','Osteoporosis — Screening & Management','medium','mcq','A 70-year-old woman on long-term prednisolone for RA presents with acute severe back pain. X-ray reveals a vertebral compression fracture. Which additional medication is most important to optimise her bone health?','["A. Calcitonin nasal spray only","B. Increase prednisolone dose for pain relief","C. Calcium and vitamin D supplementation plus bisphosphonate","D. Start raloxifene and avoid calcium supplements"]','C','Glucocorticoid-induced osteoporosis (GIOP) is common with prednisolone use. Patients on long-term steroids should receive calcium (1000–1300 mg/day) and vitamin D (200–800 IU/day) supplementation alongside a bisphosphonate. The Ghana STG also recommends this combination for moderate-to-high fracture risk.','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','Osteoporosis — Screening & Management','hard','mcq','Which serious adverse effect is associated with long-term bisphosphonate use and requires counselling before dental procedures?','["A. Avascular necrosis of the femoral head","B. Pathological fractures of long bones","C. Renal tubular acidosis","D. Osteonecrosis of the jaw"]','D','Osteonecrosis of the jaw (ONJ) is a rare but serious complication of bisphosphonate therapy, particularly with IV bisphosphonates used in oncology. Risk increases with dental extractions and implants. Patients should have dental review before starting bisphosphonates.','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','Osteoporosis — Screening & Management','hard','mcq','Which agent is preferred for treatment of severe postmenopausal osteoporosis with very high fracture risk, as it works as an anabolic agent stimulating bone formation?','["A. Teriparatide (PTH 1-34)","B. Alendronate","C. Denosumab","D. Raloxifene"]','A','Teriparatide is a recombinant PTH analogue that acts anabolically to stimulate osteoblasts, increasing bone formation. It is preferred for severe osteoporosis with very high fracture risk or failure of antiresorptive therapy. It is given as a daily subcutaneous injection for up to 2 years.','universal',false,'{year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','Osteoporosis — Screening & Management','easy','mcq','Which of the following patient groups is at highest risk for osteoporotic fractures?','["A. Young athletic males","B. Postmenopausal women, especially those who are thin and smoke","C. Premenopausal women","D. Obese men over 50"]','B','The highest-risk group for osteoporosis includes postmenopausal women (oestrogen loss), low body weight (less mechanical loading), smoking (reduces oestrogen and bone density), family history of fracture, and advanced age. Obesity is actually somewhat protective via mechanical loading.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','Osteoporosis — Screening & Management','medium','mcq','A 65-year-old woman on alendronate for 5 years reports new thigh pain. X-ray reveals a transverse fracture of the femoral shaft. Which complication of bisphosphonate therapy does this represent?','["A. Stress fracture from osteoporosis","B. Osteosarcoma","C. Atypical femoral fracture","D. Paget''s disease of bone"]','C','Atypical femoral fractures (subtrochanteric or shaft transverse fractures with cortical beaking) are associated with long-term bisphosphonate use. They may be bilateral. Guidelines recommend bisphosphonate holiday after 3–5 years in lower-risk patients to reduce this risk.','universal',true,'{year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','Osteoporosis — Screening & Management','hard','mcq','Denosumab inhibits which molecular pathway to reduce osteoclast activity?','["A. Sclerostin","B. Parathyroid hormone receptor","C. Oestrogen receptor","D. RANKL (receptor activator of NF-κB ligand)"]','D','Denosumab is a monoclonal antibody against RANKL, which is required for osteoclast differentiation, activation, and survival. Blocking RANKL reduces bone resorption. Unlike bisphosphonates, denosumab is safe in severe renal impairment and is given as 60 mg SC every 6 months.','universal',false,'{year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','SLE — Diagnosis & Management','easy','mcq','Which antibody is most specific for systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE)?','["A. Anti-double-stranded DNA (anti-dsDNA)","B. Anti-cyclic citrullinated peptide (anti-CCP)","C. Anti-Ro/SSA","D. Rheumatoid factor"]','A','Anti-dsDNA antibodies are highly specific for SLE (>95% specificity) and correlate with disease activity, particularly lupus nephritis. ANA is sensitive but not specific. Anti-Ro is associated with Sjogren''s and neonatal lupus. Anti-CCP is specific for RA.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','SLE — Diagnosis & Management','easy','mcq','A 28-year-old woman presents with a butterfly-shaped facial rash, joint pains, fatigue, and proteinuria. Which diagnosis is most likely?','["A. Rheumatoid arthritis","B. Systemic lupus erythematosus","C. Dermatomyositis","D. Rosacea with arthralgia"]','B','The triad of malar rash (butterfly distribution sparing nasolabial folds), polyarthralgia, and nephritis in a young woman is classic for SLE. The Ghana STG notes SLE predominantly affects women of childbearing age. Dermatomyositis causes heliotrope rash and proximal muscle weakness.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','SLE — Diagnosis & Management','medium','mcq','Which drug is recommended as baseline therapy in all patients with SLE without contraindications?','["A. Methotrexate","B. Cyclophosphamide","C. Hydroxychloroquine","D. Mycophenolate mofetil"]','C','Hydroxychloroquine (HCQ) is the cornerstone of SLE management, reducing disease flares, preventing organ damage, reducing thrombosis risk, and improving survival. It should be prescribed to all SLE patients unless contraindicated (retinopathy risk — annual ophthalmological review required).','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','SLE — Diagnosis & Management','medium','mcq','A 32-year-old woman with SLE develops nephrotic syndrome. Renal biopsy shows diffuse proliferative lupus nephritis (WHO Class IV). What is the most appropriate induction therapy?','["A. Hydroxychloroquine alone","B. Low-dose prednisolone plus methotrexate","C. Azathioprine plus hydroxychloroquine","D. High-dose prednisolone plus cyclophosphamide or mycophenolate mofetil"]','D','Class IV lupus nephritis (diffuse proliferative — the most severe class) requires aggressive induction therapy with high-dose corticosteroids combined with cyclophosphamide or mycophenolate mofetil (MMF). MMF is preferred in Black patients. Maintenance therapy uses MMF or azathioprine.','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','SLE — Diagnosis & Management','medium','mcq','The Ghana STG 2017 notes that SLE can lead to which haematological complications?','["A. Leucopaenia, lymphopaenia, and thrombocytopaenia","B. Polycythaemia and thrombocytosis","C. Leucocytosis and eosinophilia","D. Megaloblastic anaemia exclusively"]','A','The Ghana STG 2017 lists leucopaenia, lymphopaenia, and thrombocytopaenia as haematological complications of SLE. These may result from autoimmune destruction (anti-platelet, anti-lymphocyte antibodies), hypersplenism, or bone marrow suppression — and all feature in the ACR classification criteria.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','SLE — Diagnosis & Management','hard','mcq','Which complement levels characteristically fall during active SLE disease flares?','["A. C3 and C4 — rise during flares","B. C3 and C4 — fall during flares due to consumption","C. C5a and C5b — fall due to renal excretion","D. Total complement is unaffected in SLE"]','B','During active SLE, complement proteins C3 and C4 are consumed by immune complex deposition, causing their levels to fall. Low C3/C4 combined with rising anti-dsDNA titres are useful biomarkers of a lupus flare, particularly nephritis. Monitoring these guides treatment escalation.','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','SLE — Diagnosis & Management','hard','mcq','An SLE patient on hydroxychloroquine for 7 years reports deteriorating vision. Which complication is most likely?','["A. Cataracts from corticosteroid use","B. Optic neuritis from CNS lupus","C. Retinal toxicity from hydroxychloroquine","D. Glaucoma from steroid eye drops"]','C','Hydroxychloroquine can cause cumulative retinal toxicity (bull''s-eye maculopathy), leading to irreversible visual loss. Risk increases with cumulative dose, renal impairment, and treatment >5 years. Annual ophthalmological review is mandatory. Dose should not exceed 5 mg/kg/day.','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','SLE — Diagnosis & Management','medium','mcq','Which cardiac complication of SLE involves sterile endocarditis affecting valves (typically mitral and aortic) with risk of thromboembolism?','["A. Dressler''s syndrome","B. Rheumatic fever","C. Infective endocarditis","D. Libman-Sacks endocarditis"]','D','Libman-Sacks endocarditis is a non-infectious, verrucous endocarditis on both surfaces of valves (particularly mitral), associated with antiphospholipid antibody syndrome in SLE. It carries risks of thromboembolism, valve regurgitation, and superimposed infection.','universal',false,'{year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','SLE — Diagnosis & Management','easy','mcq','A young woman with SLE develops painless oral ulcers. This feature contributes to ACR classification criteria. How many criteria must be met for SLE classification?','["A. ≥4 of 11 criteria","B. ≥3 of 11 criteria","C. ≥5 of 11 criteria","D. All 11 criteria must be present"]','A','The 1997 ACR criteria for SLE classification require ≥4 of 11 criteria (serially or simultaneously). These include: malar rash, discoid rash, photosensitivity, oral ulcers, arthritis, serositis, renal disorder, neurological disorder, haematological disorder, immunological disorder, and positive ANA.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','SLE — Diagnosis & Management','hard','mcq','Antiphospholipid syndrome (APS) in SLE is characterised by which clinical and laboratory triad?','["A. Proteinuria, haematuria, and low C4","B. Arterial or venous thrombosis, pregnancy loss, and persistently positive antiphospholipid antibodies","C. Joint erosions, anti-dsDNA, and serositis","D. Haemolytic anaemia, thrombocytosis, and anti-Smith antibodies"]','B','Antiphospholipid syndrome (primary or secondary to SLE) is defined by: arterial/venous thrombosis and/or pregnancy morbidity, with persistent positivity (≥12 weeks apart) of lupus anticoagulant, anticardiolipin IgG/IgM, or anti-β2-glycoprotein I antibodies.','universal',true,'{year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','Seronegative Spondyloarthropathies','easy','mcq','Which HLA type is most strongly associated with ankylosing spondylitis?','["A. HLA-DR4","B. HLA-DR2","C. HLA-B27","D. HLA-B8"]','C','HLA-B27 is present in 90–95% of patients with ankylosing spondylitis (AS), though it is not diagnostic alone (8% of the general population carries HLA-B27 without AS). It is part of the seronegative spondyloarthropathy group, which also includes reactive arthritis, psoriatic arthritis, and IBD-associated arthritis.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','Seronegative Spondyloarthropathies','easy','mcq','A 25-year-old man presents with insidious-onset low back pain improving with exercise but not rest, morning stiffness >1 hour, and buttock pain alternating sides. What is the most likely diagnosis?','["A. Mechanical low back pain","B. Lumbar disc prolapse","C. Fibromyalgia","D. Ankylosing spondylitis"]','D','Inflammatory back pain in ankylosing spondylitis is characterised by: onset <45 years, insidious onset, improvement with exercise (not rest), morning stiffness >30–60 minutes, and alternating buttock pain. X-ray may show bilateral sacroiliitis. ESR/CRP are elevated.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','Seronegative Spondyloarthropathies','medium','mcq','Which ocular complication is most commonly associated with ankylosing spondylitis and requires urgent ophthalmological referral?','["A. Acute anterior uveitis (iritis)","B. Scleritis","C. Episcleritis","D. Posterior uveitis"]','A','Acute anterior uveitis (iritis) occurs in 25–30% of patients with AS, presenting with acute painful red eye, photophobia, and blurred vision. It is the most common extra-articular manifestation of AS. Urgent slit-lamp examination and topical corticosteroids are required to prevent synechiae and glaucoma.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','Seronegative Spondyloarthropathies','medium','mcq','For a patient with ankylosing spondylitis inadequately controlled on two NSAIDs at full dose, what is the next recommended treatment step?','["A. Methotrexate","B. TNF inhibitor (e.g., etanercept or adalimumab)","C. Hydroxychloroquine","D. Sulfasalazine alone"]','B','Per ASAS/EULAR guidelines, patients with axial SpA failing two NSAIDs should escalate to biologic DMARDs — TNF inhibitors (etanercept, adalimumab, certolizumab, golimumab) are first-line biologics. Methotrexate has minimal evidence for axial disease. IL-17 inhibitors (secukinumab) are alternatives.','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','Seronegative Spondyloarthropathies','medium','mcq','Psoriatic arthritis is best classified as which type of arthritis based on Harrison''s description?','["A. A purely axial arthritis identical to ankylosing spondylitis","B. A non-inflammatory degenerative arthritis in psoriasis patients","C. An inflammatory musculoskeletal disease with both autoimmune and autoinflammatory features","D. A septic arthritis secondary to skin infections in psoriasis"]','C','Harrison''s describes psoriatic arthritis as an inflammatory musculoskeletal disease with both autoimmune and autoinflammatory features. It presents in multiple patterns: asymmetric oligoarthritis, symmetric polyarthritis (RA-like), DIP involvement, axial disease, and arthritis mutilans.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','Seronegative Spondyloarthropathies','hard','mcq','Which radiological finding on plain X-ray of the sacroiliac joints is considered diagnostic of established ankylosing spondylitis?','["A. Periarticular osteopenia","B. Unilateral sacroiliac joint narrowing","C. Subchondral erosions at L5/S1","D. Bilateral grade II–IV sacroiliitis"]','D','The modified New York criteria for AS require bilateral sacroiliitis grade II–IV OR unilateral grade III–IV on X-ray, combined with clinical criteria (inflammatory back pain, limited lumbar motion, limited chest expansion). MRI detects early sacroiliitis (bone marrow oedema) before X-ray changes appear.','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','Seronegative Spondyloarthropathies','hard','mcq','Reactive arthritis classically follows which type of infection and presents with which triad?','["A. Urogenital or enteric infection; arthritis, urethritis, conjunctivitis (Reiter''s syndrome)","B. Staphylococcal skin infection; septic monoarthritis, rash, fever","C. Viral upper respiratory infection; symmetric polyarthritis, rash, lymphadenopathy","D. Dental infection; TMJ arthritis, cervical lymphadenopathy, fever"]','A','Reactive arthritis (formerly Reiter''s syndrome) follows urogenital (Chlamydia) or enteric (Salmonella, Shigella, Campylobacter, Yersinia) infections. The classic triad is arthritis, urethritis (or cervicitis), and conjunctivitis. It is HLA-B27 associated and usually self-limiting (3–12 months).','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','Seronegative Spondyloarthropathies','medium','mcq','Enthesitis — inflammation at tendon/ligament insertion points — is a hallmark of which group of disorders?','["A. Crystal arthropathies","B. Seronegative spondyloarthropathies","C. Septic arthritis","D. Connective tissue disorders"]','B','Enthesitis (inflammation at insertion of tendons, ligaments, and joint capsule into bone) is a pathological hallmark of spondyloarthropathies. Common sites include Achilles tendon insertion, plantar fascia origin, and at iliac crest, ischial tuberosity. It presents as localised pain and tenderness.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','Seronegative Spondyloarthropathies','easy','mcq','Which skin finding in psoriatic arthritis most closely correlates with nail involvement and DIP joint disease?','["A. Guttate psoriasis","B. Pustular psoriasis","C. Nail pitting and onycholysis","D. Erythrodermic psoriasis"]','C','Nail pitting and onycholysis in psoriatic arthritis are strongly associated with DIP joint involvement. The DIP joints (and nail beds) share synovial and vascular anatomy with the overlying nail apparatus — making DIP arthritis and nail disease almost invariably co-occurring.','universal',false,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','Seronegative Spondyloarthropathies','hard','mcq','IL-17A inhibitors (e.g., secukinumab) are used in spondyloarthropathies. What is their principal mechanism?','["A. Inhibit B-cell activation and antibody production","B. Block TNF-α binding to its receptor","C. Inhibit JAK1/JAK3 signalling","D. Neutralise IL-17A, reducing neutrophil recruitment and bone erosion"]','D','Secukinumab is a monoclonal antibody that neutralises IL-17A, reducing downstream neutrophil recruitment, inflammation, and osteoclast-mediated bone erosion. It is approved for AS, PsA, and PsO. IL-17 is a key cytokine in spondyloarthropathy pathogenesis, making this a targeted mechanism.','universal',false,'{year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','Septic Arthritis — Emergency Management','easy','mcq','What is the most common causative organism in native joint septic arthritis in adults?','["A. Staphylococcus aureus","B. Streptococcus pneumoniae","C. Neisseria gonorrhoeae","D. Pseudomonas aeruginosa"]','A','Staphylococcus aureus is the most common organism causing septic arthritis in adults. The Ghana STG also identifies S. aureus and S. epidermidis as accounting for the majority of acute orthopaedic infections. Gonococci are most common in sexually active young adults.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','Septic Arthritis — Emergency Management','easy','mcq','According to the Ghana STG 2017, what is the case fatality of septic arthritis if diagnosed late?','["A. 2%","B. 11%","C. 5%","D. 25%"]','B','The Ghana STG 2017 explicitly states that septic arthritis has a case fatality of 11%. Late diagnosis and poor treatment lead to joint damage and death. The STG emphasises that a hot swollen joint should be regarded as septic arthritis until proven otherwise.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','Septic Arthritis — Emergency Management','medium','mcq','According to the Ghana STG 2017, what is the first-line empiric antibiotic regimen for acute septic arthritis/osteomyelitis in adults?','["A. Amoxicillin + Clavulanic Acid IV alone","B. Gentamicin IV alone","C. Cloxacillin IV 500 mg 6-hourly combined with Ciprofloxacin IV 400 mg 8–12 hourly","D. Clindamycin oral only"]','C','The Ghana STG 2017 first-line treatment for acute orthopaedic infections in adults is Cloxacillin IV 500 mg 6-hourly for 2–4 weeks PLUS Ciprofloxacin IV 400 mg 8–12 hourly for 14 days. This combination covers the predominant pathogens (Staphylococci and Gram-negative organisms).','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','Septic Arthritis — Emergency Management','medium','mcq','A patient with sickle cell disease develops acute osteomyelitis. Which additional organism must be covered empirically beyond Staphylococcus?','["A. Pseudomonas aeruginosa","B. Mycobacterium tuberculosis","C. Candida albicans","D. Salmonella species"]','D','Salmonella species are a common cause of osteomyelitis and septic arthritis in patients with sickle cell disease. The Ghana STG 2017 specifically lists Salmonella as a causative organism in sickle cell patients and includes Ciprofloxacin (which covers Salmonella) in the treatment protocol for this group.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','Septic Arthritis — Emergency Management','medium','mcq','In a patient with penicillin allergy who develops acute septic arthritis, what antibiotic regimen does the Ghana STG 2017 recommend?','["A. Clindamycin IV 300 mg 6-hourly plus Gentamicin IV","B. Amoxicillin IV and metronidazole","C. Ceftriaxone IV alone","D. Oral co-amoxiclav"]','A','For penicillin-sensitive patients, the Ghana STG 2017 recommends Clindamycin IV 300 mg 6-hourly for 7 days PLUS Gentamicin IV 40–80 mg 8-hourly for 14 days. Gentamicin renal and auditory monitoring (BUE, creatinine, weekly auditory exams) is required during treatment.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','Septic Arthritis — Emergency Management','hard','mcq','The Ghana STG 2017 states that empiric antibiotic treatment for acute septic arthritis should be started without necessarily waiting for which investigation result?','["A. FBC and ESR","B. Culture and sensitivity report","C. X-ray of the affected joint","D. MRI of the joint"]','B','The Ghana STG explicitly states: "It is important to cover empirically with appropriate antibiotics without necessarily waiting for the culture and sensitivity report." Delay in treatment increases risk of joint destruction and mortality. Blood and aspirate cultures should be taken before antibiotics but results should not delay treatment.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','Septic Arthritis — Emergency Management','hard','mcq','Which synovial fluid WBC count threshold strongly suggests septic arthritis?','["A. >500 cells/µL","B. >2000 cells/µL","C. >50,000 cells/µL with >90% PMNs","D. >100 cells/µL"]','C','A synovial WBC count >50,000 cells/µL with >90% polymorphonuclear leucocytes (PMNs) is highly suggestive of septic arthritis, though counts can be lower in immunosuppressed patients. Gram stain and culture confirm the diagnosis. Gout and pseudogout can occasionally produce counts in this range.','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','Septic Arthritis — Emergency Management','medium','mcq','Which non-pharmacological intervention is critical in the management of septic arthritis to prevent joint destruction?','["A. Physiotherapy and strengthening exercises during acute phase","B. Application of a compression bandage","C. NSAIDs injected directly into joint","D. Joint washout (arthroscopic or open drainage)"]','D','Joint washout (aspiration, arthroscopic lavage, or open surgical drainage) is essential to reduce bacterial load, remove destructive enzymes, and prevent cartilage damage. The Ghana STG lists ultrasound-guided aspiration and incision/drainage as non-pharmacological treatments. Serial aspiration may be required.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','Septic Arthritis — Emergency Management','easy','mcq','A child with acute osteomyelitis in Ghana has sickle cell disease. According to the Ghana STG, when should referral to an orthopaedic specialist occur?','["A. If there is collection of pus and no improvement in 36 hours","B. Only if fever persists beyond 2 weeks","C. After 5 days of failed oral antibiotics","D. Only if blood cultures are positive"]','A','The Ghana STG 2017 states: Refer to an orthopaedic specialist if there is collection of pus and no improvement in 36 hours. This reflects the urgent surgical drainage required and the high risk of chronicity and complications in sickle cell patients with osteomyelitis.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','Septic Arthritis — Emergency Management','hard','mcq','Chronic osteomyelitis may arise from mycobacterial or fungal infection in addition to bacterial causes. Which surgical procedure is specifically used to remove dead bone in chronic osteomyelitis?','["A. Arthroplasty","B. Sequestrectomy","C. Arthroscopy","D. Osteotomy"]','B','Sequestrectomy — surgical removal of the sequestrum (dead bone) — is a key intervention in chronic osteomyelitis. Dead bone acts as a nidus for persistent infection and prevents antibiotic penetration. The Ghana STG lists sequestrectomy and implant removal as surgical options for chronic osteomyelitis.','universal',true,'{year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','Back Pain — Clinical Approach','easy','mcq','Which of the following is a red flag symptom in a patient presenting with back pain requiring urgent investigation?','["A. Pain worse with prolonged sitting","B. Morning stiffness improving with exercise","C. Bilateral leg weakness with bladder dysfunction","D. Pain radiating to the posterior thigh above the knee"]','C','Red flags in back pain requiring urgent assessment include: cauda equina syndrome (bilateral leg weakness, saddle anaesthesia, bladder/bowel dysfunction), fever with weight loss (infection/malignancy), history of malignancy, age >50, nocturnal pain, and pain not improving with rest.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','Back Pain — Clinical Approach','easy','mcq','Cauda equina syndrome is a neurosurgical emergency. Which symptom is most pathognomonic?','["A. Unilateral sciatica with L4 dermatome involvement","B. Bilateral knee pain with reduced reflexes","C. Upper limb paraesthesia","D. Saddle anaesthesia with urinary retention"]','D','Saddle anaesthesia (numbness in the perineal/inner thigh/perianal area) with urinary retention (overflow incontinence) or loss of anal tone constitutes cauda equina syndrome. It requires emergency MRI and urgent surgical decompression. Delay risks permanent bladder, bowel, and sexual dysfunction.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','Back Pain — Clinical Approach','medium','mcq','A 45-year-old man has right-sided sciatica with L5 nerve root compression. Which clinical finding is expected on examination?','["A. Weakness of foot dorsiflexion and great toe extension","B. Absent ankle jerk reflex","C. Absent knee jerk reflex","D. Loss of sensation in the medial calf"]','A','L5 nerve root compression causes: weakness of great toe extension (extensor hallucis longus) and foot dorsiflexion, sensory loss over the dorsum of the foot and lateral leg. The ankle jerk is preserved (S1 root). L4 compression causes absent/reduced knee jerk.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','Back Pain — Clinical Approach','medium','mcq','What is the most appropriate initial management for acute non-specific mechanical low back pain (<6 weeks)?','["A. Immediate MRI spine and neurosurgical referral","B. Reassurance, staying active, simple analgesia, and physiotherapy","C. Bed rest for 2 weeks with opiate analgesia","D. Epidural corticosteroid injection"]','B','The evidence-based management of acute non-specific LBP is: reassurance (good prognosis — 90% resolve in 6 weeks), encourage activity (bed rest is counterproductive), simple analgesia (NSAIDs, paracetamol), and physiotherapy/exercise. Imaging and specialist referral are not required without red flags.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','Back Pain — Clinical Approach','medium','mcq','Which clinical test is most useful for diagnosing lumbar nerve root compression (sciatica) in the clinical setting?','["A. Schober''s test","B. FABER test","C. Straight leg raise (Lasègue''s test)","D. Trendelenburg test"]','C','The straight leg raise (SLR/Lasègue''s test) is the key clinical test for lumbar disc herniation causing sciatica. Pain reproduced between 30–70° of elevation (radiating below the knee in the dermatomal distribution) is positive. >90% sensitivity for L4-L5 or L5-S1 disc herniation. Schober''s tests lumbar flexion in AS.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','Back Pain — Clinical Approach','hard','mcq','Which feature most reliably distinguishes inflammatory back pain (spondyloarthritis) from mechanical back pain?','["A. Severity of pain at rest","B. Radiation of pain below the knee","C. Onset during sleep","D. Improvement of pain and stiffness with exercise and NSAIDs"]','D','Inflammatory back pain (AS/SpA) classically improves with exercise and worsens with rest — the opposite of mechanical back pain. NSAIDs provide significant relief in inflammatory back pain. Additional features: onset <45 years, insidious onset >3 months, alternating buttock pain, good response to NSAIDs.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','Back Pain — Clinical Approach','hard','mcq','Spinal stenosis causes neurogenic claudication. How does this differ from vascular claudication?','["A. Neurogenic claudication is relieved by sitting/flexing the spine; vascular claudication by standing still","B. Both are relieved by standing still","C. Vascular claudication is worsened by going downhill","D. Neurogenic claudication is associated with absent peripheral pulses"]','A','Neurogenic claudication from lumbar spinal stenosis causes leg pain/heaviness/paraesthesia worsened by walking and extension of the spine (relieved by sitting, leaning forward, cycling — which opens the spinal canal). Vascular claudication is relieved by standing still. Peripheral pulses are normal in neurogenic claudication.','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','Back Pain — Clinical Approach','medium','mcq','A 68-year-old man with known prostate cancer presents with progressive back pain worse at night, not relieved by rest, and new lower limb weakness. Which diagnosis must be urgently excluded?','["A. Facet joint degeneration","B. Metastatic spinal cord compression","C. Lumbar disc herniation","D. Ankylosing spondylitis"]','B','Night pain, progressive neurological deficit, and known malignancy are red flags for metastatic spinal cord compression (MSCC). This is an oncological emergency requiring urgent MRI and high-dose dexamethasone followed by radiotherapy or surgical decompression. The most common primary sites are prostate, breast, lung, and myeloma.','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','Back Pain — Clinical Approach','easy','mcq','In which vertebral level does the spinal cord typically end in adults, below which lies the cauda equina?','["A. T10","B. L3","C. L1–L2","D. L5–S1"]','C','The spinal cord ends at the conus medullaris at approximately L1–L2 in adults. Below this level, the vertebral canal contains the cauda equina (bundle of nerve roots). A disc prolapse at L1–L2 or below compresses nerve roots rather than the cord directly, causing lower motor neuron signs.','universal',false,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','Back Pain — Clinical Approach','hard','mcq','Facet joint (zygapophyseal) pain as a cause of back pain is best characterised by which clinical feature?','["A. Radiating pain below the knee in a dermatomal pattern","B. Bilateral leg paraesthesia worsened by walking","C. Morning stiffness >1 hour with improvement on exercise","D. Axial low back pain with referred buttock/thigh pain not below knee, worsened by extension/rotation"]','D','Facet joint pain is axial, may radiate to buttocks and posterior thigh (not below knee), and is worsened by extension and rotation of the spine. There are no neurological deficits. It is distinct from discal pain (worsened by flexion/sitting) and radicular pain (dermatomal radiation below knee).','universal',false,'{year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','MSK Emergencies','easy','mcq','Which clinical sign is NOT part of the classic "6 Ps" of acute compartment syndrome?','["A. Pyrexia (fever)","B. Pain out of proportion to injury","C. Paraesthesia","D. Pallor"]','A','The 6 Ps of compartment syndrome are: Pain (out of proportion, worsened by passive stretch), Pressure (elevated compartment pressure), Paraesthesia, Pallor, Pulselessness, and Paralysis. Pyrexia is not among the 6 Ps; it may suggest infection (which can co-exist).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','MSK Emergencies','easy','mcq','What is the definitive treatment for acute compartment syndrome?','["A. IV mannitol to reduce intracompartmental oedema","B. Emergency fasciotomy","C. Elevation of the limb and NSAIDs","D. Hyperbaric oxygen therapy"]','B','Emergency fasciotomy — surgical incision of the fascial compartment(s) — is the only definitive treatment for acute compartment syndrome. It must be performed within 6 hours of symptom onset to prevent irreversible muscle necrosis and permanent disability. All constricting dressings/casts must be removed immediately.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','MSK Emergencies','medium','mcq','A 22-year-old man is found unconscious after being trapped under rubble for 6 hours. He is resuscitated and urine appears dark red-brown. Serum CK is 45,000 U/L. What is the most likely diagnosis?','["A. Acute haemolytic transfusion reaction","B. Haematuria from bladder trauma","C. Rhabdomyolysis","D. Hepatic failure from crush injury"]','C','Rhabdomyolysis following prolonged muscle compression (crush injury) presents with myoglobinuria (dark red-brown urine), massively elevated CK (>10,000 U/L), and risk of acute kidney injury. The pigmented urine is myoglobin (not haemoglobin). Aggressive IV hydration is the cornerstone of treatment.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','MSK Emergencies','medium','mcq','What is the primary mechanism by which rhabdomyolysis causes acute kidney injury?','["A. Immune complex deposition in glomeruli","B. Renal artery vasospasm","C. High uric acid causing urate nephropathy","D. Myoglobin tubular toxicity and tubular obstruction in acidic urine"]','D','Myoglobin is directly nephrotoxic and precipitates in renal tubules, especially in acidic urine, causing acute tubular necrosis. Treatment includes aggressive IV fluid hydration (urine output target >200 mL/hr), urinary alkalinisation with bicarbonate to prevent myoglobin precipitation, and monitoring for hyperkalaemia and AKI.','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','MSK Emergencies','medium','mcq','Fat embolism syndrome (FES) most commonly occurs after which type of injury?','["A. Long bone fractures (femur, tibia) and pelvic fractures","B. Soft tissue lacerations","C. Spinal cord injuries","D. Joint dislocations without fracture"]','A','Fat embolism syndrome occurs most commonly after long bone fractures (especially femur and tibia) and pelvic fractures, as fat globules from the marrow enter the circulation. It presents 24–72 hours post-injury with the classic triad: hypoxaemia, neurological dysfunction, and petechial rash.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','MSK Emergencies','hard','mcq','The classic triad of fat embolism syndrome includes petechiae in which distribution?','["A. Lower limbs and dorsum of feet","B. Conjunctivae, axillae, and upper chest/neck","C. Palmar surfaces","D. Around the umbilicus"]','B','The petechial rash of fat embolism syndrome classically appears in the conjunctivae, axillae, and upper chest and neck. It is caused by fat globules obstructing dermal capillaries. This rash, combined with hypoxaemia (PaO2 <60 mmHg) and neurological features (confusion, seizures), defines the syndrome.','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','MSK Emergencies','hard','mcq','At what compartment pressure threshold, or what pressure differential, is fasciotomy definitively indicated in compartment syndrome?','["A. Compartment pressure >10 mmHg","B. Compartment pressure >60 mmHg regardless of BP","C. Compartment pressure >30 mmHg, or ΔP (diastolic BP minus compartment pressure) <30 mmHg","D. Compartment pressure >20 mmHg with any neurological symptoms"]','C','Fasciotomy is indicated when compartment pressure exceeds 30 mmHg, or when the differential pressure (ΔP = diastolic BP − compartment pressure) is <30 mmHg. The ΔP threshold is more clinically useful as it accounts for perfusion pressure. Absolute pressure of >30 mmHg is the commonly cited threshold.','universal',true,'{year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','MSK Emergencies','medium','mcq','Which electrolyte abnormality is the most immediately life-threatening complication of rhabdomyolysis?','["A. Hyponatraemia","B. Hypocalcaemia","C. Hyperphosphataemia","D. Hyperkalaemia"]','D','Hyperkalaemia is the most immediately life-threatening complication of rhabdomyolysis. Massive release of intracellular potassium from damaged muscle into the circulation, combined with AKI-impaired excretion, can cause fatal cardiac arrhythmias. ECG monitoring and urgent treatment of hyperkalaemia are essential.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','MSK Emergencies','easy','mcq','Pain on passive stretch of the muscles within the compartment is the EARLIEST and most reliable sign of which MSK emergency?','["A. Acute compartment syndrome","B. Deep vein thrombosis","C. Rhabdomyolysis","D. Fat embolism syndrome"]','A','Pain on passive stretch of muscles in the affected compartment is the earliest and most reliable clinical sign of acute compartment syndrome. For example, passive dorsiflexion of the toes causes pain in anterior tibial compartment syndrome. This sign precedes the "late" signs of pulselessness and pallor.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','MSK Emergencies','hard','mcq','Which complication of rhabdomyolysis and massive muscle damage can cause paradoxical hypocalcaemia in the acute phase?','["A. Parathyroid hormone suppression from hyperphosphataemia","B. Calcium sequestration in damaged muscle (calcium phosphate deposition) and hyperphosphataemia","C. Increased renal calcium excretion from myoglobin","D. Hypoalbuminaemia reducing protein-bound calcium"]','B','Hypocalcaemia in rhabdomyolysis results from two mechanisms: (1) calcium sequestration in damaged muscle cells as calcium phosphate deposits, and (2) hyperphosphataemia (from cell lysis) causing reciprocal hypocalcaemia. This hypocalcaemia is usually not treated unless symptomatic, as calcium may later deposit in soft tissues.','universal',false,'{year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','Perioperative MSK Management','easy','mcq','Which class of anticoagulants is most commonly used for VTE prophylaxis following total hip or knee replacement?','["A. Warfarin alone","B. Unfractionated heparin IV infusion","C. Low molecular weight heparin (LMWH) or direct oral anticoagulants (DOACs)","D. Aspirin alone"]','C','LMWH (enoxaparin) and DOACs (rivaroxaban, apixaban) are the recommended pharmacological VTE prophylaxis agents following major orthopaedic surgery. Mechanical prophylaxis (compression stockings, pneumatic devices) is used as adjunct. Duration is 10–14 days for knee replacement, up to 35 days for hip replacement.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','Perioperative MSK Management','easy','mcq','A patient with RA on methotrexate is scheduled for elective total knee replacement. What is the standard recommendation regarding methotrexate perioperatively?','["A. Stop methotrexate 3 months before surgery","B. Double the methotrexate dose preoperatively","C. Switch to prednisolone and stop methotrexate 1 week before surgery","D. Continue methotrexate through the perioperative period — evidence supports continuation"]','D','Current evidence and ACR/EULAR guidelines support continuing methotrexate through the perioperative period for elective orthopaedic surgery in RA, as it does not increase infection risk and stopping it risks disease flare. Biologics (TNF inhibitors) are typically held for 1 drug half-life before surgery.','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','Perioperative MSK Management','medium','mcq','A patient on long-term prednisolone 10 mg daily for RA is undergoing major hip surgery. What perioperative steroid management is required?','["A. Administer hydrocortisone stress dosing (50–100 mg IV at induction and continue for 24–48 hours)","B. Stop prednisolone 48 hours before surgery","C. Continue regular dose only — no additional steroids required","D. Increase prednisolone to 20 mg orally preoperatively only"]','A','Patients on chronic corticosteroids have hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis suppression and cannot mount an adequate cortisol stress response to surgery. Hydrocortisone stress dosing (50–100 mg IV at induction, then 25–50 mg 8-hourly for 24–48 hours) prevents adrenal crisis perioperatively.','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','Perioperative MSK Management','medium','mcq','Which biologic DMARD class should be withheld before elective surgery in RA due to infection risk, and for approximately how long?','["A. Hydroxychloroquine — withheld 6 months preoperatively","B. TNF inhibitors — withheld for approximately 1 drug half-life (typically 2–4 weeks) before surgery","C. Methotrexate — withheld 4 weeks preoperatively","D. Sulfasalazine — withheld 1 week preoperatively"]','B','TNF inhibitors (e.g., etanercept, adalimumab) impair immune defence and increase surgical infection risk. ACR/EULAR guidelines recommend withholding them for approximately 1 drug half-life before elective surgery (variable: etanercept ~1 week, adalimumab ~2 weeks, infliximab ~4–8 weeks depending on dosing interval).','universal',true,'{year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','Perioperative MSK Management','medium','mcq','DVT prophylaxis duration after total hip arthroplasty recommended by current guidelines is approximately how long?','["A. 5–7 days","B. 10–14 days","C. 28–35 days","D. 90 days"]','C','Current guidelines (NICE, ACCP) recommend pharmacological DVT prophylaxis for up to 28–35 days following total hip arthroplasty. For total knee arthroplasty, 10–14 days is typical. Extended prophylaxis is warranted for hip surgery given the higher VTE risk due to prolonged immobility and pelvic venous stasis.','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','Perioperative MSK Management','hard','mcq','A patient with RA and cervical spine atlantoaxial subluxation requires emergency anaesthesia. What is the key anaesthetic consideration?','["A. Use spinal anaesthesia to avoid any cervical manipulation","B. Avoid prone positioning to prevent pressure sores","C. Rapid sequence induction is preferred to minimise intubation time","D. Careful awake fibreoptic intubation to avoid cervical spine manipulation — risk of cord compression"]','D','Atlantoaxial subluxation (instability at C1-C2) in RA risks spinal cord compression during neck extension for intubation. The preferred technique is awake fibreoptic intubation with the neck kept in a neutral position. Pre-anaesthetic flexion-extension cervical X-rays are required for elective procedures in RA patients.','universal',true,'{year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','Perioperative MSK Management','hard','mcq','Which NSAIDs are typically withheld preoperatively and for what primary reason?','["A. They increase bleeding risk through reversible platelet inhibition and GI bleeding","B. They reduce bone healing and osseointegration of implants","C. They interact with anaesthetic agents causing hepatotoxicity","D. They cause intraoperative hypotension through vasodilation"]','A','NSAIDs inhibit COX-1 (reversibly for most NSAIDs — aspirin is irreversible) and reduce thromboxane A2-mediated platelet aggregation, increasing surgical bleeding risk. They also increase perioperative GI bleeding. NSAIDs are typically withheld for 5–7 days before surgery (aspirin for 7–10 days if stopped).','universal',false,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','Perioperative MSK Management','easy','mcq','What is the primary goal of perioperative DVT prophylaxis in orthopaedic surgery?','["A. Prevent wound haematoma formation","B. Prevent deep vein thrombosis and pulmonary embolism","C. Prevent prosthetic joint infection","D. Reduce postoperative anaemia"]','B','The primary goal of DVT prophylaxis in orthopaedic surgery is prevention of deep vein thrombosis and potentially fatal pulmonary embolism (PE). Major lower limb joint replacement is a high VTE-risk procedure due to venous stasis, endothelial injury, and hypercoagulable state — Virchow''s triad.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','Perioperative MSK Management','hard','mcq','A patient on warfarin for atrial fibrillation requires elective total knee replacement. What is the most appropriate perioperative anticoagulation management?','["A. Continue warfarin throughout and check INR on day of surgery","B. Switch to aspirin permanently","C. Stop warfarin 5 days preoperatively, bridge with LMWH, restart warfarin postoperatively","D. Perform surgery with INR <3 without any dose adjustment"]','C','For elective surgery, warfarin is stopped 5 days preoperatively (to allow INR to normalise to <1.5 for surgery). Bridging with therapeutic LMWH is used for high thromboembolic risk (e.g., mechanical heart valves, recent VTE). Warfarin is restarted postoperatively once haemostasis is achieved.','universal',true,'{year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','Perioperative MSK Management','medium','mcq','Which factor increases the risk of prosthetic joint infection in total joint arthroplasty, requiring prophylactic antibiotic consideration?','["A. Advanced age >60","B. Female sex","C. Preoperative physiotherapy","D. Immunosuppression (RA on DMARDs, diabetes, obesity)"]','D','Immunosuppression — from disease (RA, diabetes, obesity, steroid use) or medication (DMARDs, biologics) — significantly increases prosthetic joint infection risk. Prophylactic IV antibiotics (cefazolin) are given at induction. Dental procedures in the first 2 years post-arthroplasty require antibiotic prophylaxis in high-risk patients.','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','Crystal Arthropathy — Differential Diagnosis','easy','mcq','On polarised light microscopy of synovial fluid, calcium pyrophosphate dihydrate (CPPD) crystals appear as which of the following?','["A. Positively birefringent rhomboid-shaped crystals","B. Negatively birefringent needles","C. Non-birefringent spherical crystals","D. Strongly negatively birefringent bipyramidal crystals"]','A','CPPD crystals (pseudogout) are positively birefringent (blue when parallel to compensator axis) and rhomboid or rod-shaped. This distinguishes them from monosodium urate crystals (gout), which are negatively birefringent and needle-shaped. This microscopic distinction is critical in the crystal arthropathy differential.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','Crystal Arthropathy — Differential Diagnosis','easy','mcq','Pseudogout (CPPD arthropathy) most commonly affects which joint, distinguishing it from classic gout?','["A. First metatarsophalangeal joint","B. Knee","C. Wrist","D. Ankle"]','B','CPPD arthropathy (pseudogout) most commonly affects the knee, whereas gout classically begins in the first MTP joint (podagra). CPPD attacks also tend to be more prolonged than gout and predominantly affect older women. The Ghana STG notes "attacks persist longer and affect the knee most commonly."','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','Crystal Arthropathy — Differential Diagnosis','medium','mcq','A 70-year-old woman presents with acute knee pain and X-ray shows chondrocalcinosis (calcification of articular cartilage). What is the most likely crystal arthropathy?','["A. Gout","B. Hydroxyapatite crystal deposition disease","C. Calcium pyrophosphate dihydrate (CPPD) crystal deposition — pseudogout","D. Oxalate crystal arthropathy"]','C','Chondrocalcinosis on X-ray (calcification within fibrocartilage — menisci — and hyaline cartilage) is pathognomonic of CPPD crystal deposition. The knee is the most common joint. CPPD is confirmed by synovial fluid analysis showing positively birefringent rhomboid crystals.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','Crystal Arthropathy — Differential Diagnosis','medium','mcq','Which synovial fluid analysis finding is most useful in distinguishing septic arthritis from crystal-induced arthritis?','["A. Yellow colour of synovial fluid","B. WBC count >10,000 cells/µL","C. Elevated protein in synovial fluid","D. Gram stain and culture showing organisms, or culture-negative with crystals on polarised microscopy"]','D','The definitive distinction between septic arthritis and crystal arthropathy requires: positive Gram stain/culture (septic) versus identification of crystals on polarised microscopy (crystal arthropathy). Crucially, both can have high WBC (>50,000 cells/µL) — they can also co-exist. Waiting for culture results is appropriate but empirical antibiotics are started if sepsis suspected.','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','Crystal Arthropathy — Differential Diagnosis','medium','mcq','Which metabolic conditions are associated with secondary CPPD crystal deposition?','["A. Hyperparathyroidism, haemochromatosis, hypomagnesaemia, and hypothyroidism","B. Hyperuricaemia and obesity","C. Hypocalcaemia and vitamin D deficiency","D. Diabetes mellitus and obesity"]','A','Secondary CPPD is associated with metabolic disorders: hyperparathyroidism, haemochromatosis, hypomagnesaemia, hypothyroidism, hypophosphatasia, and gout. These conditions promote calcium crystal formation in cartilage. Investigation for these conditions is warranted, especially in younger patients with CPPD.','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','Crystal Arthropathy — Differential Diagnosis','hard','mcq','In hydroxyapatite crystal deposition disease, which clinical scenario is most characteristic?','["A. Acute knee monoarthritis with fever in an elderly woman","B. Acute periarthritis (calcific tendinitis) particularly of the shoulder rotator cuff with intense pain","C. Chronic polyarthritis of DIPs in older men","D. Sacroiliac joint pain in a young man"]','B','Basic calcium phosphate (hydroxyapatite) deposition causes calcific tendinitis, most commonly affecting the supraspinatus tendon of the rotator cuff, causing acute severe shoulder pain with X-ray calcification visible in the tendon. It can also cause Milwaukee shoulder syndrome — a destructive arthropathy in elderly women.','universal',false,'{year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','Crystal Arthropathy — Differential Diagnosis','hard','mcq','According to Harrison''s, which of the following is a distinctive clinical manifestation that can be induced by CPPD crystals and mimics neuropathic arthritis radiographically?','["A. Acute monoarthritis of the first MTP","B. Periarticular calcification of the shoulder only","C. Severe destructive arthropathy radiographically mimicking Charcot''s arthropathy","D. Sacroiliitis on MRI"]','C','Harrison''s states CPPD can induce "severe destructive disease that may radiographically mimic neuropathic arthritis (Charcot joint)." This severe CPPD arthropathy presents as progressive joint destruction, typically in knees, wrists, or hips, with X-ray showing joint space loss, fragmentation, and calcification.','universal',false,'{year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','Crystal Arthropathy — Differential Diagnosis','medium','mcq','A patient presents with a hot swollen first MTP joint. Serum uric acid is normal. Can gout be excluded?','["A. Yes — normal uric acid rules out gout","B. Yes — gout only occurs with serum uric acid >600 µmol/L","C. No — only if anti-CCP is positive can gout be excluded","D. No — serum uric acid may be normal or even low during acute gout attacks"]','D','The Ghana STG explicitly notes that gout may be present even when serum uric acid levels are normal, as levels may acutely fall during an attack. Conversely, patients with hyperuricaemia may never have gout. Definitive diagnosis requires joint aspiration and identification of negatively birefringent MSU crystals.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','Crystal Arthropathy — Differential Diagnosis','easy','mcq','Tophi in chronic gout are deposits of monosodium urate crystals. Which of the following is a common location for tophus formation?','["A. Helix of the ear, olecranon bursa, and Achilles tendon","B. Cornea of the eye","C. Nail beds","D. Dermis of the back"]','A','Tophi (urate crystal aggregates) characteristically deposit in: helix of the ear, fingers, toes, prepatellar and olecranon bursae, and Achilles tendon. The Ghana STG lists these sites. Tophi represent chronic urate deposition and indicate longstanding uncontrolled gout requiring urate-lowering therapy.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES ('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Musculoskeletal System','Clinicals','Crystal Arthropathy — Differential Diagnosis','hard','mcq','In the synovial fluid analysis table for crystal arthropathies, which combination is correct?','["A. Gout: positive birefringence, rhomboid crystals; CPPD: negative birefringence, needles","B. CPPD: needle-shaped, positively birefringent; Gout: rhomboid, negatively birefringent","C. Gout: needle-shaped, negatively birefringent; CPPD: rhomboid, positively birefringent; Septic: no crystals, positive culture","D. Septic: crystals present; Gout: no birefringence"]','C','The correct crystal differential: Gout = needle-shaped, negatively birefringent MSU crystals (yellow parallel to compensator); CPPD = rhomboid or rod-shaped, weakly positively birefringent (blue parallel); Septic arthritis = no crystals on polarised microscopy, positive Gram stain/culture. These can co-exist.','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');
